                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "As the third game in the trilogy, the Demon War continued to have the same issues as it's predecessors: constant failure due to high skill checks, forced relationships, and choices largely not impacting the story.\r\n\r\nIn this game, I felt like the failures were worse because despite having high points in a skill, checks were often failed regardless, often combining with another skill that is difficult to build or some story element forcing the failure for a reason that doesn't make sense. \r\n\r\nAdditionally, there were a lot of points in the game that caused you to lose a lot of points in multiple stat fields, with multiple times you lose ten points in every category of a field. Even without losing points, skill checks would be very, very difficult to pass, and it felt like the game was making things harder for no reason constantly.\r\n\r\nThere were often new stats for different sections of the game as well, such as boats and armies, which is frustrating because any time spent building up those stats is time wasted because it is soon obsolete.\r\n\r\nOutcomes of choices were very difficult to predict. It felt like failures were forced, and sometimes they happened without skill checks. The story was fine, but it felt like my choices didn't affect it, and nothing about it was very compelling.",
